{align:justify|In 1997, a brilliant engineer designed and built a most unusual pig-slaughtering machine: feed a live pig into its mouth, and within minutes skin, bristles, flesh, bones, blood, head, and all would emerge, each portion perfectly cut and sorted.}
{align:justify|When the machine was assembled, on the day of its trial run all the company leaders and the designing engineer's family came to watch.}
{align:justify|The engineer started the machine himself as a demonstration. Confidently he flipped the switch. As he turned to step away, his clothing unexpectedly caught in the machine, as if someone were dragging him back; while he tugged with his hands, the machine's mouth seemed to exert a tremendous suction, drawing him straight inside. The onlookers, witnessing the horror, were stunned with terror.}
{align:justify|Someone rushed to cut the power — but too late. The engineer was no longer alive: for within minutes, true to his design, the slaughter machine swiftly fulfilled its mission, dividing his body into perfect portions exactly as its inventor had programmed — blood, flesh, bones, head, and innards all emerging neatly arranged in a flash. His colleagues could only stand and stare. His family wailed to heaven, all blindsided by what had just happened, unable to imagine that he himself would be the first test subject of his slaughter machine.}
{align:justify|Here we see humankind's selfishness, without a shred of mercy. Had it been a pig that fell into the machine just now, all would have cheered the successful experiment — and felt nothing like this grief.}
{align:justify|When the celebrated American writer Hemingway died, the whole world was shaken and mourned — for who could understand how so renowned a talent could take his own life, and with a gun at that? People found it utterly baffling.}
{align:justify|In fact, Hemingway in life loved hunting and shot countless animals with his gun — so in the end he used a gun to kill himself. In terms of karmic retribution, is this not the very answer?}
